I'm sorry you felt that way. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to normalize HTML tags using regular expressions:

**1. Understanding Normalization**

Normalizing HTML tags means removing any special characters, formatting, or attributes that may be embedded in the tag name. This ensures that all tags have a consistent and predictable format.

**2. Removing Special Characters**

You can remove special characters from the start and end of the tag name using a regular expression: `[^\w]`.

```java
String tagName = " <span>";
tagName = tagName.replaceAll("[^\\w]", "");
```

This will replace all non-word characters (such as spaces, punctuation, and special characters) with an empty string.

**3. Removing Formatting**

To remove formatting from the tag name, you can use a regular expression that matches any whitespace followed by a word character or non-whitespace character: `\s+[\w]`.

```java
String tagName = "   <span>     ";
tagName = tagName.replaceAll("\\s+\\W+", "");
```

This will replace all one or more whitespace characters with an empty string, effectively removing any leading or trailing whitespace.

**4. Removing Attributes**

To remove attributes from the tag name, you can use a regular expression that matches any attribute name followed by an equals sign: `=\s*`.

```java
String tagName = "id=\"foo\"";
tagName = tagName.replaceAll("\\s*=\\s*", "");
```

This will replace all occurrences of one or more whitespace characters between attribute names and their values with an empty string.

**5. Converting to Space-Only**

To convert the tag name to a space-only format, you can use a regular expression that matches any non-whitespace character: `[^\\w]`.

```java
String tagName = "foo";
tagName = tagName.replaceAll("[^\\w]", " ");
```

This will replace all non-word characters with a single space.

**6. Example Use Case**

Here's an example of how you can use these methods to normalize HTML tags:

```java
public static String normalizeTagName(String tagName) {
    tagName = tagName.replaceAll("[^\\w]", " ");
    tagName = tagName.replaceAll("\\s+\\W+", " ");
    tagName = tagName.replaceAll("\\s*=\\s*", "");
    return tagName;
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println(normalizeTagName("id=\"foo\"")); // Output: " foo"
}
```

By following these steps, you can ensure that your HTML tags have a consistent and predictable format, making it easier to work with them in your code.
